Most advice about `AGENTS.md` is written by people describing what a good file should look like. This guide goes the other way: it reads 20 real files that maintainers committed to some of the most-starred repositories on GitHub, quotes them, measures them, and reports what they have in common. The 20 repos span Next.js, PyTorch, Rust, Kubernetes, Node.js, Bun, Ruff, VS Code, n8n, LangChain and more, and together they hold about 3,800 lines of agent instructions.

The short result: there is no single shape. The files run from 7 lines (VS Code) to 532 lines (Next.js). Some are build manuals, some are almost pure AI-contribution policy, and a growing number are routers that point the agent at `SKILL.md` files. If you want to write your own, skip ahead to the [checklist](#best-practices-checklist) and the [starter template](#starter-template). If you want to know why those recommendations look the way they do, read the examples first.

{% youtube id="6w88NVf2_lY" title="How I Write My AGENTS.md Files - Best Practices (NeuralNine)" /%}

*An independent walkthrough of AGENTS.md best practices. We confirmed the video exists and its title through YouTube oEmbed on November 27, 2026; we did not use its claims as evidence for anything below.*

## The short answer

Across the 20 files (all fetched from GitHub on November 27, 2026), these are the findings that matter for anyone writing an `AGENTS.md` file:

- **Length varies by a factor of 76.** The median file is about 190 lines (roughly 12 KB). Eight files are under 100 lines, eight are between 100 and 300, and four are over 300.
- **Commands are common but not universal.** 15 of 20 files include at least one concrete build, test or lint command. Five (Excalidraw, Node.js, Svelte, VS Code and llama.cpp) include no build, test or lint command and point to `CONTRIBUTING.md`, a Copilot instructions file or contributor policy instead.
- **"Do not" rules beat descriptions.** The files that read best contain traps ("never hand-edit generated files") rather than architecture tours. Zed's file says this outright, and we quote it below.
- **AI-contribution policy is now part of the genre.** Six files (PyTorch, Rust, Transformers, Kubernetes, Node.js, llama.cpp) spend real space on when an agent may or may not act, what must be disclosed, and who is responsible for the result.
- **Skills are the new split point.** 11 of 20 files tell the agent to load named skills or `SKILL.md` folders, usually to keep the root file short.

Quick picks from the 20, as of November 27, 2026:

- **Best to copy for a large monorepo:** [Next.js](#nextjs) and [n8n](#n8n) (commands, test loops, PR hygiene, skills).
- **Best short file:** [Kubernetes](#kubernetes) (39 lines: constraints, contributor rules, commands, style).
- **Best AI-contribution policy:** [PyTorch](#pytorch), [Node.js](#nodejs) and [llama.cpp](#llamacpp).
- **Best use of skills routing:** [OpenAI Agents SDK](#openai-agents-sdk) and [Supabase](#supabase).
- **Best advice on maintaining the file itself:** [Zed](#zed).
- **Best minimal pointer file:** [VS Code](#vs-code) (7 lines that delegate to Copilot instructions).

{% callout type="info" %}
"agent.md examples" is a common spelling of the query, but the filename in every repo below is `AGENTS.md` (plural). For which tools read which filename, and how `AGENTS.md` relates to `CLAUDE.md`, `SKILL.md` and Cursor rules, see our companion guide [AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md vs SKILL.md vs Cursor rules](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules).
{% /callout %}

## What an AGENTS.md file is, and who reads it

`AGENTS.md` is a plain Markdown file at the root of a repository (and optionally in subfolders) that gives coding agents the context a README does not: how to build, how to test, what conventions to follow, what to avoid. The project site at [agents.md](https://agents.md/) describes it as "a simple, open format for guiding coding agents" and says it is "used by over 60k open-source projects" (that is the site's own claim; we did not verify the number). Its own explanation of why the file exists is that README files are for humans, while `AGENTS.md` holds "the extra, sometimes detailed context coding agents need".

{% figure src="https://agents.md/og.png" alt="Social preview card for agents.md, the site describing the AGENTS.md format" caption="The agents.md project's own social preview image. Source: agents.md" /%}

The format has no required fields. OpenAI's Codex documentation, fetched November 27, 2026, describes the one mechanical behavior worth knowing: Codex builds an instruction chain by reading `AGENTS.md` files from the project root down to the current directory, concatenating them, and stopping once the combined size reaches `project_doc_max_bytes`, which defaults to 32 KiB. In each directory it checks `AGENTS.override.md` first, then `AGENTS.md`. Other tools document their own limits and precedence rules; the point for authors is that a very large root file competes with nested files for a finite budget.

The format came out of a cross-vendor effort. These two posts from August 2025 announce it from the people involved:

{% x id="1957924964105179455" /%}

{% x id="1957945824404729997" /%}

OpenAI's developer account has since pointed at `AGENTS.md` as a place for repository-specific review rules, which matters later when we look at the "Code Review Rules" sections in Ruff and the OpenAI Agents SDK:

{% x id="2079627776722981348" /%}

## How we picked the 20 files

We started from roughly 55 popular repositories, fetched the root `AGENTS.md` of each through the GitHub contents API, and kept the 20 that satisfied all of these:

1. The file exists at the repository root today (we fetched it on November 27, 2026 and recorded the size, line count and last commit touching that path).
2. The repository is widely used (the smallest has 30,902 stars, the largest 229,614).
3. The file contains real instructions rather than a one-line redirect, with two deliberate exceptions: VS Code (7 lines, kept as the minimal-pointer example) and Excalidraw (20 lines, kept as a "traps only" example).
4. Together the 20 cover different domains: web frameworks, languages and runtimes, ML libraries, infrastructure, developer tools and AI agent frameworks.

We did not pick by quality ranking. "Top" here means high-star and real. Two caveats on what you are about to read. First, star counts and file contents change daily; every number is dated. Second, the stars are for the repository, not the file: a famous repo can have a thin file and vice versa. That is why the table below sorts by nothing in particular and the per-repo sections explain what each file does well.

