## Best for X: which example to read first

| If you want to... | Read this file | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Write your first `AGENTS.md` in 20 minutes | Kubernetes or Angular | 39 lines each, every line a decision |
| Document a big monorepo | Next.js or n8n | Commands, test loops, PR rules and skills |
| Set rules for AI contributions | PyTorch, Node.js or llama.cpp | Disclosure, accountability, prohibited actions |
| Stop agents from wasting reviewer time | Transformers or Rust | Explicit "do not open this PR" rules |
| Route to skills | OpenAI Agents SDK or Supabase | Trigger-to-skill tables and lists |
| Keep the file clean over time | Zed | Rules for adding rules |
| Reuse existing instructions | VS Code or Bun | Pointer file and symlink |
| Teach one tricky workflow | Excalidraw | A single worked procedure with known pitfalls |
| Write about commands and checks | Playwright, Bun or opencode | Order of operations and guarded rules |

## How we did this

- **Selection and fetching.** We scanned about 55 popular repositories with the GitHub CLI and API on November 27, 2026, fetched the root `AGENTS.md` for each through the contents API, and kept the 20 featured. Stars, default branch, size, line count and last-commit date come from `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>`, the contents API and the commits API for the path `AGENTS.md`.
- **Excerpts.** Every blockquote is copied from the raw file text we downloaded, trimmed to a sentence or two. Ellipses are not used inside quotes. Where we paraphrase, we do not use quotation marks.
- **Counts.** Line counts are from `wc -l` equivalents on the raw files. The theme counts in the heading table were produced by matching heading text outside code fences, then reading the results; the pattern table was filled in by reading each file, and "Some" marks borderline cells.
- **Bun's symlink.** We checked the git tree entry for `AGENTS.md` in `oven-sh/bun`: mode 120000, size 9 bytes, with `CLAUDE.md` alongside it at 18,159 bytes. We also confirmed Bun's content by reading it through the contents API.
- **What we did not do.** We did not run any agent against these files, measure whether a rule changes agent behavior, or test how any tool truncates a large file. Statements about what makes a file "good" are our editorial judgment from reading them, not an experiment. We did not verify the agents.md site's "60k" claim, and we attribute it to the site.
- **Media.** The two YouTube videos and four X posts were verified with oEmbed and the X syndication endpoint (details in the sources).

## FAQ

### What is an AGENTS.md file?

It is a Markdown file at the root of a repository (and optionally in subfolders) that tells AI coding agents how to work on the project: build and test commands, conventions, and things to avoid. The [agents.md](https://agents.md/) site calls it "a simple, open format for guiding coding agents" and compares it to a README for agents.

### What should go in an AGENTS.md file?

The files in this guide agree on a few things: commands (build, test, lint), hard constraints, testing rules specific to the repo, PR and commit rules, and, increasingly, an AI-contribution policy and a list of skills. The best files include only what an agent could not infer from reading the code. See the [checklist](#best-practices-checklist).

### How long should an AGENTS.md be?

There is no fixed answer. In the 20 files we read, the median is about 190 lines, the shortest is 7 and the longest is 532. A practical ceiling is the size budget of your heaviest tool: Codex, for example, stops adding files once the combined size reaches 32 KiB by default (`project_doc_max_bytes`). Next.js's file, at 30,556 bytes, fits under that alone but leaves little room for anything else.

### Is it AGENTS.md or agent.md?

`AGENTS.md`, plural. "agent.md" is a common misspelling and a common search term, so people searching for "agent.md examples" are almost always looking for `AGENTS.md` examples. All 20 files here use the plural name. Some tools do accept other names through configuration; Codex, for example, supports `project_doc_fallback_filenames`.

### Does Claude Code read AGENTS.md?

Our companion guide documents the current behavior: [AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md vs SKILL.md vs Cursor rules](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules) covers which tools read which file, with sources. In practice several repos in this guide sidestep the question: Bun symlinks `AGENTS.md` to `CLAUDE.md`, and Transformers has `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` import `AGENTS.md`.

### Can I have more than one AGENTS.md in a repo?

Yes. Codex's documentation says it reads files from the project root down to the current working directory, joins them with blank lines, and lets files closer to the working directory override earlier guidance because they appear later. It checks `AGENTS.override.md` before `AGENTS.md` in each directory. In this guide, Zed's file refers to per-crate `AGENTS.md` files, Supabase's refers to nested `AGENTS.md` files for some apps, and n8n links to a nested Playwright `AGENTS.md`.

### What is the difference between AGENTS.md and a skill?

`AGENTS.md` holds always-on guidance that every session should see. A skill (`SKILL.md` in a folder) holds a procedure that loads when it is relevant. Next.js puts it this way in its own file: "Use skills for conditional, deep workflows. Keep baseline iteration/build/test policy in this file." Our [comparison guide](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules) goes deeper.

### Should an AGENTS.md include an AI-contribution policy?

If your project gets unsolicited AI-generated pull requests, the maintainers of PyTorch, Rust, Node.js, llama.cpp, Kubernetes and Transformers all decided yes. Their files differ in tone, but share three elements: the human is accountable, AI use must be disclosed (`Assisted-by` in Node.js and llama.cpp, a disclosure note in Kubernetes), and the agent must not act autonomously on the public repo.

### How do I make an AGENTS.md that agents actually follow?

We did not test agent compliance, so this is a reading of the examples rather than a measurement. The files that get specific are the ones that name the exact command, say when to run it, and spell out what not to do in plain imperatives ("NEVER", "Do not"). Rules that restate what a linter enforces add length without adding signal. Zed's file recommends requiring that new rules be non-obvious, repeatedly encountered and specific.

### Can an AGENTS.md be generated automatically?

Tools can draft one. In the catalog, [agents-md](https://agentskills.codes/skills/agents-md) and [deepinit](https://agentskills.codes/skills/deepinit) both generate files from the codebase. Auto-generated drafts tend to fill the file with architecture descriptions, which is the pattern Zed's file warns against, so review and cut before committing. We did not run either skill for this guide.

### Do I need to keep AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md in sync?

Not if you make one the source of truth. Transformers states in its file that `AGENTS.md` is the only copy and that `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` import it. Bun uses a symlink from `AGENTS.md` to `CLAUDE.md`. [codex-cli-bridge](https://agentskills.codes/skills/codex-cli-bridge) can generate one from the other if you prefer to maintain both.

### Is it safe to put commands in AGENTS.md?

Commands are the most common content, but treat the file as public if the repo is public. Next.js and Supabase both warn about this: never print secret values, and keep absolute production metrics out of PR descriptions, issues and comments. Do not put credentials, internal URLs or private metrics in the file.

### How current are these examples?

All 20 files were fetched on November 27, 2026. Thirteen of the 19 non-symlink files had a commit touching them in the preceding 30 days, so some will have changed by the time you read this. Use the linked GitHub blob URLs for the latest version.

